WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.650
- You are going to learn how to get

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.020
right to the point in a conversation.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.540
And today we're here with Chris Fenning.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.517
He's the author of the
book, "The First Minute:

00:00:09.517 --> 00:00:12.690
"How to Start Conversations
That Get Results."

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.370
Thanks for being with us today, Chris.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.650
- Hi, Alex, it's great to be here.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.000
- If you'd like to connect with Chris,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.360
you can look at his website,
his LinkedIn profile,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.150
of course, I'll link
to his book on Amazon,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.160
and you can check out his
YouTube page, as well.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.050
I'll put links to all of those things

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.750
in the description below the video.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.590
So Chris, one of the most
common questions I get is, Alex,

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.500
when you're out in the
work world consulting,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.690
what's the biggest communication problem

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.340
that you hear about?

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.060
And I would say the number
one complaint I hear about is

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.310
that people say, the people around me

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.370
are really long-winded.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.550
So what's your perspective
on that complaint?

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.240
- I think that is, yeah,
one of the highest,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.730
if not the highest issues
that I see, as well.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.490
What I find interesting
about it is it's always

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.440
other people who are long-winded.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.540
Ask a room full of people
if they know someone

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.070
who struggles to get to the point,

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.470
they're all going to nod and say yes.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.200
Ask those same people if they
are the person who struggles

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.330
to get to the point,
oh no, no, it's not me,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.230
it's always other people who
have that particular challenge.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.030
- That's right, I find that one

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.270
of the biggest misunderstandings
about communication

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.030
is that people who can just talk a lot

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.620
think they're good communicators,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.840
but it's almost exactly the opposite.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.390
So today what we're going
to do is learn how to get

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.610
right to the point in a
way that will get results.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.460
And we're going to do this
by framing the conversation,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.650
which Chris talks about in his book,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.720
and we're gonna talk
about three key questions

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.420
that'll help us frame the
conversation the right way.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.890
Right when we start a conversation,

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.170
people are often asking themselves,

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.360
what are you talking about?

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.920
So Chris, why does this happen

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.500
and what can we do about
it to answer that question?

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.010
- We've all been there
a minute or two minutes

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.560
into a conversation and
we're still thinking,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.880
what are you talking about?

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.250
What is this, what does it relate to?

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.340
And the reason this happens
is the person who's speaking

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.550
hasn't defined the context
for the conversation.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.530
You don't know whether they're
talking about a meeting

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.240
next week, or a client problem,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.640
or something that just was a funny story.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.950
When we don't provide context,

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.290
the people we're talking
to struggle to focus

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.150
and they're spending their
time trying to work out

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.280
what is it we're actually talking about.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.640
The great thing is context
is easy to provide.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.410
In just three or five words,

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.600
you can tell the person you're talking to.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.520
what you're talking about.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.790
Something like, I have an
update on next week's meeting,

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.280
or I'd like to talk to you
about the Jefferson account,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.340
or can I talk to you
about Robin's pay rise?

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.670
Those few words tell you,
I could say, hey, Alex,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.860
I wanna talk to you about
our meeting next week.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.050
You now know everything else I'm going

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.000
to say is about the meeting next week

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.180
and it's not about lunch, or
reports, or anything else.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.040
- That's why we buy a book, right?

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.570
We see a title like yours

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.790
and we say, oh, the first minute,
I need to know about that,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.160
I know what this book is about now.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.890
And if we are only
thinking that in our heads

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.700
but never verbalize that until
later in the conversation,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.070
that's a really demanding
way to start a conversation

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.650
to make them guess what
it is we're talking about.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.750
So you want to be really
upfront about that.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:19.980
It takes a little reflection

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.570
before you start the conversation,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.190
but it's pretty easy to get to.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.940
And I noticed the samples that
you used were very concise.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.310
It's just one short little sentence

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.750
that does a lot of heavy lifting.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.630
So let's say we tell them
what we're talking about.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:37.950
The second question that most
listeners ask themselves is,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.520
why are you talking to me
about this specifically?

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.250
So why do people always ask
themselves this question

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.770
and what can we do to
make sure we don't leave

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.840
that question unanswered?

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.350
- Yes, this is the situation
where someone has come up

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.030
to you and you understand
what the topic is,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:54.863
you know the context,

00:03:54.863 --> 00:03:56.820
but they've been talking
for a few minutes,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.850
you have no idea whether
you're being asked a question,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.150
is this just them venting
about a situation,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.770
do you have to take an action?

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.780
And the reason we think those things

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.850
and we have that question of
why are you talking to me,

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.230
why are you telling me this,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.420
it's because the person speaking

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.570
hasn't made the intent clear.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.100
Now, there's some science behind this

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.960
in the way our short-term memories work,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.110
that until our brain knows what to do

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.930
with a piece of information,
it struggles to process it

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.540
because different parts of
our brain work on listening

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.550
to a funny story or answering a question.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:33.000
And until we know the intent,
until we know the purpose

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.610
for that conversation, our
brains are stuck in a loop

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.740
saying, why are you telling
me this, what do I do with it,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.660
what do I do with it,
what do I do with it?

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.900
And that's why we get
stuck in that question of,

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:43.290
well, why are you talking to me?

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.450
Now, the easy way to avoid this is

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:49.500
after providing context, you
literally tell the other person

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:50.970
what you want them to do

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.800
with the information you're giving them.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.800
For example, can I talk to
you about next week's meeting?

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.400
I need your help to identify
the people to bring.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.840
Or I want you to talk to you

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.000
about this particular client's account,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.100
I need your help approving a refund.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.370
So we've got, I need your
help, I want your input.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.380
Heads up, something is about to happen.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.890
These short phrases that tell
the other person what to do

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.960
make the intent really clear

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.080
and they will focus on everything
else that you have to say.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.160
- Excellent examples.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.500
So we all ask this question
whether we're watching

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.930
a presentation, we're in a group meeting,

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.120
one-on-one conversation,
we all ask some version

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.890
of Why are you telling me this?

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.780
So I have this guy I
know, I wouldn't call him

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.190
a close friend, but I know him,

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.100
and he frequently will interrupt you

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.550
in the middle of a conversation.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.500
And he asks a little bit rough,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.440
he'll say, what's this got to do with me?

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.820
And I would not imitate his
style, but he is being honest.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.800
He doesn't understand why you're talking

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.180
to him about this in particular.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.120
And I think everybody asks
some version of that question,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.670
hopefully a little more politely.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:54.600
But they're always wondering,

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.370
what do I have to do
with this information?

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:57.510
Why are you talking to me about it?

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.590
How can I take action?

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.360
Maybe they're even asking, how can I help?

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.640
But unless you tell them that directly,

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.830
they're still going to wonder.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.840
So that brings us to the third question,

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.090
and that is, you've
already created context,

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:10.860
you've already signaled intention,

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.370
and the last part is they're
asking, what's your point?

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.590
What is the point of this conversation?

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.900
So tell us how we can get to that point

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.090
in the conversation really clearly.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.580
- Oh yes, clearly and quickly.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.760
There is very little that's
more frustrating at work

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.460
than listening to somebody
go on and on and on

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.190
and thinking, what is your point?

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.400
I understand what you want
from me, but what's the point?

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.780
And that is caused by missing a headline

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:42.120
from the start of your message.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.680
One of the best ways to imagine this

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.140
or to to experience this
outside of a conversation

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.840
is pick up a magazine, or newspaper,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.340
or look at an article online

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.010
and start reading from paragraph seven.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.990
How long will it take you to work out

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.760
what the article is about?

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.530
You could even read the entire thing

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.830
from paragraph seven onwards
and still not be quite sure

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.380
what the overall article or message is.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.380
Compare that to starting with a title

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.320
and a really clear first
line in the article.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.750
If that's there and that's
what you've read first,

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.290
everything else after that
makes a lot more sense.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.710
If that's true in magazines,
it's true in conversation.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.510
When you start talking to someone,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.190
don't start on paragraph seven.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.480
Don't start in the detail.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.270
Don't start with backstory or evidence.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.520
Start with a headline,
say this is the thing

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:30.810
I want to talk to you about.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:33.030
So we've already given
some examples of this.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:34.410
I snuck them in a little earlier.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.020
So one example would be,
for context, I'd like

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.820
to talk about the Jefferson's account.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.250
The intent is I need your help.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.230
And the key message is,
I would like your help

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.300
with approving a refund

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.700
because we've had some late deliveries.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.070
So the key message is
we need to refund them

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.830
or we Want to refund them because
we've had late deliveries.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.900
There's a lot more in that conversation

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.670
about why are they late and
what have we done about it,

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:00.360
how much is the refund, but
the person you're talking to

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:01.620
will know and understand

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.510
what that conversation
is going to be about

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:05.850
because it's been framed,
they've got the context,

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:07.740
they've got that intent,
and that key message,

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:11.130
the headline, has set up
the rest of the conversation

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.990
really clearly for them.

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:14.640
- I love it, excellent examples.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:18.240
So in three concise
sentences, you have created

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.930
an effective and productive foundation

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.400
for the rest of the conversation.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.580
If you skip these three
sentences, they're gonna be trying

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.250
to answer these questions for themselves,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:30.810
they're gonna be jumping to conclusions,

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.210
and it's going to create a very demanding

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:36.240
and confusing conversation
until you answer these.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:38.220
So start on the right foot,

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.710
answer these three
questions in this order,

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:42.120
and you're off to the races.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.360
Now, in terms of next steps,
we have these three tips.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.780
What can people do with these
three tips moving forward?

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.130
- It's a simple method.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.770
As with a lot of simple
methods, the difficulty is

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.500
how do you apply it and
apply it consistently?

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.920
And one thing that we can
all do is if we are about

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:02.550
to have a conversation with
someone, take 30 seconds

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.620
and just write down, you
could do this mentally,

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:07.830
but writing it down helps,
just jot down the answers

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:09.150
to those three questions.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.430
Either say, my context is
this, my intent is this,

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:13.020
and the key message is that,

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.870
or you can answer the question
of, what is this about,

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.300
why am I talking to them,
and what's the point?

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:21.090
And a single sentence,
three to five words for each

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:25.110
of them is enough to really
set up that conversation.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:27.030
So take that moment to prepare

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:29.100
before any important conversation,

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.780
and it will really, really help.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:33.690
- Wonderful, thank you so
much, Chris, for being here.

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.280
I just wanna remind viewers,

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.100
you can check out Chris's book on Amazon.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.010
There are links to his
LinkedIn, his website,

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.860
and his YouTube channel in the
description below the video.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.920
He's got amazing and helpful
content just like this.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.770
Thanks for being here with us, Chris.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.740
- Thank you, Alex, it's been a pleasure.

